add(298, 'This project develops a web application for Caro on a 15 × 15 board under Freestyle rules and uses the game to study adversarial search. The application combines a React interface, a PixiJS board and a Rust engine compiled to WebAssembly, with search executed in a Web Worker. The report explains board representation, legal moves, win detection, heuristic evaluation, candidate selection, Minimax and Alpha-Beta pruning. It also examines hashing, transposition tables, quiescence and iterative deepening in separate reference and legacy implementations. The recorded native engine experiment contains 816 search measurements, including 408 paired Minimax and Alpha-Beta comparisons and 820 state-restoration checks. Separate records examine agreement between the JavaScript adapter and the WebAssembly binary, browser Worker responses and selected interface interactions. The reference experiments show that fewer visited nodes can coincide with longer execution time when move ranking adds work. They also identify immediate tactics lost by a hard candidate cap and an incorrect score caused by reusing cached entries after the root player changes. These results connect search algorithms to an interactive application and show why candidate policy, state restoration and cache context must be specified alongside search depth.', 'Condense three inventory-like paragraphs into a coherent self-contained abstract with concrete results and no inflated claim.', research+'/engine_verification.json', ref+'/README.md', project+'/CaroGame/src/game/wasm/worker.ts')
add(1072, '', 'Merge the abstract into one paragraph; remove duplicated implementation inventory.', 'Abstract consolidated at paragraph 298')
