add(7417, 'Evidence — Source code explains implementation mechanisms; experiment records provide results for the stated inputs and environment.', 'Replace audit instruction with a clear reading convention.', research+'/README.md')

add(8183, 'The project includes a playable web application and a separate website for explaining its algorithms. React provides the interface, PixiJS renders the board, and GameController coordinates accepted moves, match state and AI requests. Move selection combines a TypeScript tactical check with a Rust/WebAssembly search executed in a Worker. The repository also contains an earlier TypeScript search module, native benchmark programs and weekly project reports. The application, benchmarks and teaching examples share the game domain but execute through different entry points. [1] [2] [3] [22] [24] [26] [33]', 'Remove the unsupported claim that independent legacy, native and browser modules form one cohesive executable architecture; standardize PixiJS terminology.', project+'/CaroGame/src/app/GameController.ts', project+'/CaroGame/src/game/ai/AIController.ts')
add(8871, 'The practical problem is to select a legal move while preserving the state of an interactive match. Search must leave its input board unchanged, follow the selected win rule and return a result that belongs to the active round. Reset, Undo and asynchronous computation make these requirements part of the algorithm integration. The report therefore studies move quality together with the controller checks that determine whether an AI response can be committed.', 'Replace repeated cautionary meta-commentary with the concrete system problem.', project+'/CaroGame/src/app/GameController.ts', project+'/CaroGame/src/game/wasm/GomokuWasmEngine.ts')
add(9469, 'The first objective is to define the game rules and state transitions. The application uses a 15 × 15 board and the Freestyle win condition: a contiguous line of at least five stones wins, including longer lines. The report explains the last-move scan and the assumptions that make it sufficient. The second objective is to describe move selection through its inputs, evaluation policy, candidate set, recursive decisions, state restoration and computational cost. [4]', 'Make objectives direct and remove redundant framing without changing the game contract.', project+'/CaroGame/src/game/core/WinRules.ts')
add(10071, 'The third objective is to explain how the algorithms connect to the application: board ownership, guarded move commitment, history replay into WebAssembly and rejection of stale responses. The fourth objective is to evaluate search under controlled conditions. The experiments compare Minimax with Alpha-Beta on the same candidate tree, separate move-ranking work from recursive search visits, and examine whether selective candidate policies retain immediate winning and blocking moves.', 'State the objective positively and replace evidence-policing language with actual comparison methods.', research+'/engine_verification.json', ref+'/results/cap_loss.json')
add(10649, 'Four research questions guide the report. RQ1 concerns deterministic board updates and Freestyle win detection. RQ2 concerns how heuristic evaluation, candidate selection and adversarial recursion produce a move. RQ3 examines the effects of pruning, ordering and caching on search results and computational work. RQ4 concerns state consistency when native search components are used through an asynchronous browser interface. Chapter 5 relates each question to the corresponding implementation and experimental records.', 'Use parallel research-question language and avoid implying formal or universal answers from finite experiments.', research+'/README.md', ref+'/README.md')
add(11358, 'The published source reference is tducn110/basicproject at revision 7e1e2d51321a4e6b2e26493c34f0a485d4de39cc. CaroGame contains the application source and its WebAssembly package; present contains the teaching website. The weekly folders, benchmark and research contain documentation and experimental records. The Rust baseline in research/current_2026_10_23/baseline_engine is an earlier implementation. The modified engine used for the 23 October experiments is identified by source hashes and selected excerpts in the dated research directory. [1] [5] [6] [44]', 'Correct the baseline/current-engine identity: archived board.rs is not the source of the active modified binary.', research+'/README.md', research+'/provenance.json', research+'/SOURCE_EXCERPTS.md')
add(11849, 'Four implementations are distinguished throughout the analysis: the Rust/WebAssembly engine used by the application, the archived Rust baseline, the legacy TypeScript bot, and the native Rust reference library used by the separate research harness. An algorithm or measurement is attributed to the implementation that supplies it. [6] [7] [8]', 'Remove circular architecture-clarity wording and define a consistent naming convention.', research+'/SOURCE_EXCERPTS.md', ref+'/README.md')
add(12281, 'The implemented scope is local two-player play, play against the computer, and interactive algorithm demonstrations. Online multiplayer, learned policies and model training are outside the project. Search strength and user experience are evaluated through the experiments and observations described in Chapter 5; broader evaluation and further development are discussed in Chapter 6.', 'Keep scope readable and move repeated absence/verification narration to the final limitations.', project+'/CaroGame/src/App.tsx', project+'/present/src/App.tsx')
add(12808, 'The method combines source inspection with analysis of recorded experiments. The implementation trace follows pointer input, coordinate conversion, move validation, board updates, terminal-state checks, AI dispatch, response validation and display updates. Search comparisons keep the rule, evaluation weights, depth and candidate policy fixed where equivalence is being tested. Primary literature supplies the theoretical basis for search and hashing, while the project references identify the code and datasets used in the analysis.', 'Replace audit instructions and source-policing rhetoric with an academic methods paragraph.', research+'/README.md', ref+'/README.md')
add(13444, 'Chapter 2 presents the data structures and algorithms. Chapter 3 explains the requirements, component responsibilities and state transitions. Chapter 4 describes the application and teaching website. Chapter 5 analyzes the native experiments, WebAssembly adapter checks, browser observations and benchmark datasets. Chapter 6 summarizes the contribution, limitations and future work. The appendices provide the repository inventory, a summary of experimental records and instructions for inspecting and building the published snapshot.', 'Remove repeated disclaimer language and accurately describe the corrected appendices.', project+'/README.md')

add(137604, 'The project implements a Caro web application and a teaching website for board representation, Freestyle rules, heuristic evaluation and adversarial search. The application connects a React interface and PixiJS board to a Rust/WebAssembly search Worker. The teaching website provides visual explanations and executable Python examples. Weekly reports and native research artifacts document the algorithmic development and its experimental evaluation. [1] [52]', 'Make the conclusion describe delivered work rather than instruct the reader how not to interpret it.', project+'/CaroGame/src/App.tsx', project+'/present/src/App.tsx')
add(138210, 'The main contribution is an implementation-level account of how game decisions depend on representation and search policy. Board updates, history and rollback support recursive exploration. Terminal utility takes precedence over the static heuristic. Minimax defines alternating decisions, and Alpha-Beta pruning preserves the value of the same selected search tree while omitting branches that cannot change it. Candidate filtering changes the tree itself; ordering changes the sequence in which its moves are examined. The reference experiments extend this analysis to Zobrist hashing, transposition-table bounds and interrupted iterative search. [44] [8]', 'Remove repetitive audit instructions and use precise algorithm terminology.', ref+'/README.md', research+'/engine_verification.json')
add(138984, 'The recorded native engine experiment reports 408 Minimax/Alpha-Beta comparisons with matching values and 820 successful restoration checks. Separate adapter and browser records exercise the connection between search and the application. In the reference experiments, move ordering reduced visited nodes on some positions, but its additional evaluation work could outweigh the saving in elapsed time. A hard candidate cap discarded immediate wins in one fixture, and cache reuse after a root-player change produced an incorrect score in another. These results show that search depth and node count alone are insufficient to assess correctness or efficiency. [43] [45] [46] [48] [16] [21]', 'Replace evaluative rhetoric with the concrete results and their implication.', research+'/engine_verification.json', ref+'/results/cap_loss.json', ref+'/results/cache_scope_probe.json')
add(139606, 'The active search is selective. Its soft tactical cap retains immediate winning and blocking completions, but deeper positional moves remain subject to the candidate limit. The configured limit falls from 15 at Hard to 8 at Master as depth rises from two to five plies. The static evaluator scores contiguous runs; gapped formations and intersecting threats depend on tactical assessment and deeper search. Persistent transposition tables and iterative-deepening budgets are studied in separate implementations rather than used by the active Rust search. [6] [18] [8]', 'Combine the actual algorithm limitations without calling fixed-depth search a complete anytime implementation.', engine+'/ai.rs::Difficulty', engine+'/candidates.rs::select_candidates', engine+'/eval.rs', engine+'/search.rs')
add(139875, 'The implementation review also identifies boundary cases for further correction. GameController starts a round before validating the first direct move request, and the core coordinate predicate checks bounds without requiring integer indices. The normal pointer path supplies integer cells, while Worker responses have a separate integer check. Drag detection compares consecutive pointer positions, which can miss a slow drag made of small steps. In the teaching website, a failed or missing Pyodide loader can leave initialization state or waiting callers unresolved. [2] [29] [30] [37]', 'Keep confirmed source defects in the final limitations; distinguish direct API exposure from normal UI behavior.', project+'/CaroGame/src/app/GameController.ts::playCell', project+'/CaroGame/src/game/core/BoardBounds.ts::isBoardCell', project+'/CaroGame/src/board/input/BoardInputController.ts::pointerMove', project+'/present/src/utils/pyodideRunner.ts::getPyodide')
add(140235, 'The measurements cover a finite corpus and specific native or browser environments. The published snapshot includes the application and its binary, but the complete modified Rust engine and the caro-core-reference dependency are absent from that Git tree, limiting reproduction from a fresh clone. Historical timing and bot-match summaries also have narrower provenance than the controlled research records. Physical-device interaction, live host integration, human usability and calibrated playing strength remain subjects for further evaluation. [1] [8] [40] [41] [44] [51]', 'Collect true reproducibility and validation limitations in the concluding chapter.', research+'/run_checks.py', ref+'/Cargo.toml', ref+'/results/provenance.json')
add(140532, 'Reproducibility is the first development priority. A complete release should include the modified engine and reference library together with their manifests, lockfiles, experiment harnesses and build commands. Each experiment should identify the source revision, toolchain, platform and binary hash. The earlier baseline should remain available for comparison under its own identity.', 'Replace numbered imperative checklist with academic future-work prose.', research+'/provenance.json', ref+'/results/provenance.json')
add(140911, 'Input validation and lifecycle handling should be strengthened at their owning boundaries. The core should require integer coordinates and validate a move before advancing the match phase. Gesture classification should use displacement from the initial press, and the Pyodide loader should settle all waiting requests and permit retry after failure. Regression cases should include invalid first moves, fractional inputs, slow drags and failed runtime initialization.', 'Prioritize source-backed defects and concrete discriminating regressions.', project+'/CaroGame/src/app/GameController.ts::playCell', project+'/CaroGame/src/game/core/BoardBounds.ts::isBoardCell', project+'/present/src/utils/pyodideRunner.ts::getPyodide')
add(141283, 'Further search evaluation should separate candidate selection from move ordering. A fixed corpus of reachable positions can compare all-legal search with radius and cap policies, including known wins, blocks and intersecting threats. Useful outputs include missed tactics, chosen moves, root values, ranking evaluations, recursive visits and elapsed time. Multiple seeds and explicit tie handling would make the comparison less dependent on one fixture.', 'Express a concrete evaluation design without implying it has already been executed.', ref+'/README.md', ref+'/results/cap_loss.json')
add(141635, 'Persistent caching requires a defined compatibility context. Future work should include rules, evaluation weights, root perspective and mate-distance treatment in the cache design, then test exact, lower and upper bounds across depths and search windows. Warm-table and cold-table comparisons should include root changes and collisions. Memory measurements should include map capacity and allocation overhead as well as entry size.', 'Retain the measured cache defect and precise future design implications in natural academic prose.', ref+'/results/cache_scope_probe.json', ref+'/results/tt_semantics.json')
add(141970, 'An anytime search could combine iterative deepening with cancellation and an explicit last-completed-result policy. Its telemetry should distinguish requested depth from completed depth and count work spent in aborted iterations. Tests should cover Reset, Undo, stale replies and Worker failure while confirming that interrupted search restores the input position.', 'Remove checklist command voice; connect the proposed feature to the observed counter limitation.', ref+'/results/budget_abort.json', project+'/CaroGame/src/game/wasm/GomokuWasmEngine.ts')
add(142300, 'Application evaluation should extend to physical target devices and the intended host environment. Repeated measurements of response latency and frame timing would complement the present automated checks. A participant study could examine control usability and perceived difficulty, while a multi-seed tournament against stated opponents could assess playing strength. Both require a documented protocol before their results can support conclusions.', 'Move unexecuted human/device/strength work to the future section without invented outcomes.', research+'/G5_browser_ui.json', project+'/benchmark/results/week4_bot_battle.json')
add(142703, 'The teaching website should remain synchronized with the implementations it explains. Illustrative programs need explicit scope, especially where a horizontal example accompanies a four-direction rule description. Charts should link to the data and generation procedure that produced them. This would let readers move from an explanation to the corresponding source or experiment without confusing a demonstration with a measurement.', 'Remove repetitive meta-instructions while retaining specific educational fidelity work.', project+'/present/src/utils/pyodideRunner.ts', project+'/present/src/components/PythonVisualizer.tsx')

# Preserve every reference and its number; parent handles first-citation renumbering.
for p in paragraphs:
    if not 143060 <= p['start'] < 159128:
        continue
    old = p['text'].removesuffix('\n')
    number = int(re.match(r'\[(\d+)\]', old).group(1))
    url = re.search(r'Available: (\S+)', old).group(1).rstrip('.')
    if number == 39:
        new = '[39] WebAssembly Community Group, “WebAssembly Specification,” release 3.0, Nov. 4, 2026. Accessed: Nov. 12, 2026. [Online]. Available: https://webassembly.github.io/spec/core/'
        reason = 'Correct the current living specification date from the official title page and standardize IEEE online-reference punctuation.'
    elif number in (12, 17):
        new = re.sub(r'\. \[Accessed: Oct\. 27, 2026\]\.$', '', old)
        reason = 'Keep verified publisher metadata and DOI; remove redundant access-date bracket and final punctuation after the online DOI URL.'
    else:
        stem = old[:old.index(' [Online]. Available:')]
        new = stem + ' Accessed: Nov. 12, 2026. [Online]. Available: ' + url
        reason = 'Standardize IEEE web-source access-date placement and URL punctuation; preserve verified title, path, revision and authorship.'
    add(p['start'], new, reason, url, ieee)

